WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:10.420
One of the systems you're likely to run into is the IBM z14. Now, you may not be working on a z14.

2
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.980
You may have something older. You may have something newer.

3
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.260
But chances are it will look something like this. The parts might look a little different. They might be rotated.

4
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.180
They might be in a slightly different place.

5
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:29.460
But for as long as I can remember, the core components of the mainframe have always looked more or less like this.

6
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.780
Starting from the right, and I'll explain why in a minute, you've got the CPC drawers.

7
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:41.660
They call them drawers because they pull open like a drawer in a nightstand. In here, you've got all the processors and all the memory.

8
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:50.300
They've also got the PCIe fan outs, which is a fancy way of saying all the cables that hook up to all the PCIe IO cards.

9
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:57.140
It's also got the flexible support processor, which gives you redundant interfaces to all the internal management network.

10
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.100
So you're never locked out. You can always log into one of these things.

11
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:06.460
There's also two DCA inverters. These provide the specific power that everything in here needs.

12
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.420
And there's two of them just in case one goes down. Down here, we've got the radiator pumps.

13
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.940
These circulate refrigerant throughout the system, keeping it down.

14
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.900
It takes the cold air being blown up from the floor and blows the hot air out the back.

15
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.420
So cold air in the bottom, hot air out the back, chilled refrigerant all throughout the system.

16
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.300
There are also water cooled systems, which tap into a site's chilled water lines.

17
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.380
Looks slightly different, but everything else is pretty much the same.

18
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.260
This right here is the PCIe IO drawer.

19
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.020
This is where all the input output devices live.

20
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.580
That's your crypto cards, network cards, compression cards and Ficon.

21
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.140
Up top over here, there's batteries.

22
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.020
There's two of them, and they're bigger than any batteries I own.

23
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.620
They're there to provide a safe shutdown.

24
00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.180
So if the power does go out, none of the transactions in flight get lost.

25
00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.620
You've got four power supplies.

26
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.420
Now, depending on how your mainframe is configured, you may have less than four.

27
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.340
But part of the reason for so many is, you guessed it, redundancy.

28
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.860
So ideally, if you've got four power supplies, you've got two of them hooked up to a power panel over here

29
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.260
and two others hooked up to a power panel over there for redundancy's sake.

30
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:22.220
And lastly, there's this display keyboard and these two servers up here.

31
00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.340
Those are called the support elements.

32
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.340
We're going to come back to that.

33
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.300
So much more on that in a minute.

34
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.060
I do want to mention that your mainframe might look like this.

35
00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.500
It might also be a single frame design.

36
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:38.100
Now, a single frame design, usually called a business class machine, is still a mainframe.

37
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:43.100
It's for environments that don't require quite as much hardware so they can fit it all into a single frame.

38
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:49.100
You might also find your mainframe, the newer ones especially, in an industry standard 19 inch rack.

39
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:55.740
That's the size cabinet that most IT equipment lives in, stuff like servers, storage devices, and networking equipment.

40
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.460
That means the mainframe doesn't have to live off in its own special area.

41
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.940
It can be right in the middle of it all.

42
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:08.220
So while the older mainframes get their own unique area, the rack metal ones get to hang out with all their other IT equipment buddies.
